    "title": "Why is Tax Day always April 15? Why you file taxes every April",
    "text": "April 13, 2026, 8:57 a.m. ETTax Day — the deadline to file 2025 income tax returns — is this Wednesday, April 15, and chances are, you've filed tax returns in April during your entire working life.While it may seem like it, April 15 hasn't always been the deadline to file tax returns.Here's why you file income tax returns in April every year.Why do you file taxes in April?Due to an act from Congress, workers have filed income tax returns in April for the past 72 years.\"April 15 became the federal income tax filing deadline in 1954, when Congress rewrote the tax code and gave taxpayers an extra month beyond the previous March 15 cutoff,\" according to LegalClarity. For 2026, the deadline falls on Wednesday, April 15, with no holiday-related shift in the calendar this year. \"The date has remained fixed in federal law for over 70 years, though it occasionally moves by a day or two when weekends or holidays interfere.\"Was Tax Day always April 15?Tax Day hasn't always fallen in April.When the IRS celebrated the 70th anniversary of Tax Day in 2025, the service explained how the tax-filing deadline was moved up a month from March to its current April date.\"The deadline was moved to April 15 from March 15 in 1955 to give taxpayers and the IRS more time to prepare and process increasingly complex returns,\" read the IRS' Tax Day celebration announcement. \"Since then, innovations in technology and customer service have transformed the tax filing experience from hand-prepared paper forms to modern e-filing and online tools that make the process faster, more secure and more accessible.\"The Tax Foundation notes changing income tax filing deadline prior to the 1955 overhaul.\"When the 16th Amendment, which allows Congress to institute the income tax, was adopted on Feb. 3, 1913, Congress chose March 1 — one year and a few dozen days later — as the deadline for filing returns,\" read the Tax Foundation's website. \"Then, with the Revenue Act of 1918, Congress inexplicably moved the date forward to March 15.\"Damon C. Williams is a Philadelphia-based journalist reporting on trending, breaking and service-related topics across the Mid-Atlantic region for the USA Today Network.",
